B.a.h(n,A.b("the investigation, in the order the comment tells it"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"crates/heaplens-daemon/tests/hook_owner_free_no_crash.rs \xb7 lines 6-22","//! ## Original finding (2026-07-21/22, `feat/stage7-injection`)\n//!\n//! The owner-freed-while-children-live workload itself (owner alloc, 8\n//! children alloc, owner free while children live, 20 rounds of\n//! alloc/realloc/free churn, children free) never crashed \u2014 it completed\n//! and printed its final marker every time, whether or not a daemon was\n//! listening. The crash was **not** in that pattern.\n//!\n//! The real, reliably-reproducing mechanism found: the target process\n//! exiting normally (falling off the end of `main`) while\n//! `RtlAllocateHeap`/`RtlReAllocateHeap`/`RtlFreeHeap` hooks were still\n//! installed \u2014 i.e. `heaplens-injector --detach` was never called before\n//! the target's own process exit \u2014 reliably terminated with exit status\n//! `0xC0000005` (STATUS_ACCESS_VIOLATION). A temporary diagnostic Vectored\n//! Exception Handler installed for that investigation never caught the\n//! fault (no log entry) \u2014 consistent with a `__fastfail`-driven crash,\n//! which bypasses SEH/VEH by design."))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,'First finding (2026-07-21 and 22, on the feat/stage7-injection branch). The reported crash was an access violation (0xC0000005) on "case 2", an owner freed while its children are still live. The investigation found that the workload itself never crashed, whether or not a daemon was listening. The crash was not in the ownership pattern. It was in the target exiting normally with the Rtl heap hooks still installed. A diagnostic vectored exception handler never fired, which the comment reads as consistent with a __fastfail, a crash path that bypasses SEH and VEH by design.'))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,'Second finding, 2026-07-26, on master. The original investigation predates the TLS/FLS fix by about two and a half hours (cf6dccc at 20:41, 8cc0f1c at 23:12 the same evening) and nobody had re-tested this scenario against the fixed build. The comment links the two: the old thread_local! crashed on first access from any thread other than the one that called LoadLibraryW, and a dying process\u2019s worker threads are exactly that population. Re-tested with the real injector: 15 of 15 clean exits for the single-threaded repro; 30 of 30 clean runs, 6,000 race attempts in total, for the FLS race repro. The wording on the second is careful: "No reproducing evidence for this theoretical gap". It does not say the hazard is impossible.'))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"Third finding, same day: a new defect, a hang and not a crash."))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"crates/heaplens-daemon/tests/hook_owner_free_no_crash.rs \xb7 lines 52-76","//! ## NEW finding, same investigation: multi-threaded exit-without-detach\n//! can HANG (not crash)\n//!\n//! `exit_without_detach_multithreaded.exe` (several worker threads\n//! *continuously* making hooked allocations in a tight loop, still live\n//! when the process exits, never joined, no detach) does not crash \u2014 but\n//! roughly 40-60% of runs (observed across ~15 attempts, several batches)\n//! the process never terminates at all. Confirmed by direct observation,\n//! not assumption:\n//! - `Process.HasExited` stays false past a 15s wait; CPU time (queried via\n//!   `Get-Process`) **plateaus** within the first couple of seconds and\n//!   never grows again, even minutes later \u2014 consistent with the worker\n//!   threads becoming *blocked*, not spinning.\n//! - The hung process resists termination by three independent\n//!   mechanisms tried in sequence: PowerShell `Stop-Process -Force`\n//!   (reports success, process persists), `taskkill /F` (reports \"no\n//!   running instance\" while `tasklist` simultaneously shows it still\n//!   running), and finally `Invoke-CimMethod ... -MethodName Terminate`\n//!   (WMI `Win32_Process.Terminate`), which did eventually succeed.\n//! - `heaplens-injector --detach` against a hung instance fails with\n//!   `OpenThread failed for worker thread <tid> in process <pid>` \u2014 the\n//!   DLL's own persistent worker thread (`attach_impl`'s spawned thread,\n//!   normally parked via alertable `SleepEx` and reused via `QueueUserAPC`\n//!   for detach) appears to be gone or invalid, breaking the normal detach\n//!   path too."))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"With several worker threads continuously allocating when the process exits, roughly 40 to 60 percent of runs never terminated. The comment lists the observations that make it a deadlock and not a spin: CPU time plateaus within seconds and never grows; the process resisted Stop-Process and taskkill /F (which reported it as not running while tasklist still listed it) and died only to WMI\u2019s Win32_Process.Terminate; and the injector\u2019s own --detach failed with an OpenThread error because the DLL\u2019s worker thread was gone."))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.b("the root cause"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"crates/heaplens-daemon/tests/hook_owner_free_no_crash.rs \xb7 lines 80-103","//! Confirmed via WinDbg Preview (`cdbX64.exe -pv`, non-invasive attach \u2014\n//! invasive attach was itself found to disturb the hang, reliably making\n//! it exit right as `DebugActiveProcess`-based attach completed, 3/3\n//! times; non-invasive attach doesn't). Symbol-resolved stack trace of\n//! the sole surviving (\"main\") thread, reproduced identically on two\n//! separate hung instances:\n//!\n//! ```text\n//! ntdll!NtWaitForAlertByThreadId\n//! ntdll!RtlWaitOnAddress\n//! KERNELBASE!WaitOnAddress\n//! heaplens_hook!std::sys::sync::mutex::futex::Mutex::lock_contended\n//! heaplens_hook!heaplens_alloc::capture::capture_stack   <- blocked here\n//! heaplens_hook!heaplens_alloc::record\n//! heaplens_hook!heaplens_hook::hook_heap_free\n//! ucrtbase!free_base\n//! ucrtbase!destroy_fls\n//! ntdll!RtlpFlsDataCleanup\n//! ntdll!LdrShutdownProcess\n//! ntdll!RtlExitUserProcess\n//! KERNEL32!ExitProcessImplementation\n//! ucrtbase!common_exit\n//! <target's own main>\n//! ```"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"A non-invasive WinDbg attach (invasive attach itself disturbed the hang: it made the process exit as the attach completed, 3 of 3 times) produced the same stack on two hung instances. The surviving thread is inside ExitProcess, running FLS cleanup, which frees a buffer; the free goes through the still-installed hook, hook_heap_free, which calls record, which calls capture_stack, which blocks on a mutex."))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"crates/heaplens-daemon/tests/hook_owner_free_no_crash.rs \xb7 lines 105-126","//! Confirmed mechanism: `RtlExitUserProcess` abruptly terminates the other\n//! worker threads without running their cleanup. If one was caught inside\n//! `capture_stack`, holding `heaplens_alloc::DBGHELP_LOCK` (a process-wide\n//! `Mutex<()>`, acquired on every hooked allocation from every thread \u2014\n//! see `capture.rs`), that lock is now orphaned permanently. The sole\n//! surviving thread's own FLS cleanup then frees a fiber-local buffer;\n//! since hooks were never disabled (no explicit detach), that free routes\n//! through `hook_heap_free` -> `record` -> `capture_stack`, which tries to\n//! acquire the same orphaned lock and blocks forever. This explains the\n//! rate difference from `fls_race_repro.exe` precisely: threads spending a\n//! larger fraction of their lifetime inside `capture_stack` (continuous\n//! looping) have proportionally higher odds of being caught mid-lock at\n//! the instant of abrupt termination than short-lived, mostly-idle ones.\n//!\n//! Fixed in `heaplens-alloc/src/capture.rs`: `capture_stack` now uses\n//! `DBGHELP_LOCK.try_lock()` instead of `.lock()` \u2014 a held lock (orphaned\n//! or merely contended, indistinguishable and both handled identically)\n//! yields an empty, unresolved capture instead of blocking. Cannot\n//! deadlock regardless of *why* the lock is unavailable. Re-validated: 25\n//! manual runs (mixed batch/individual, exit codes explicitly checked)\n//! all clean post-fix, on the same machine and binaries that showed 9/10\n//! and 7/8 hung pre-fix."))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The mechanism, step by step. capture_stack takes DBGHELP_LOCK, a process-wide mutex, on every hooked allocation of every thread, because the Windows symbol machinery is not thread safe. When the process exits, RtlExitUserProcess kills the other threads without running any cleanup. If one of those threads was inside capture_stack holding the lock at that instant, the lock is orphaned for good. The survivor then needs the same lock to record its own cleanup free, and waits forever. That explains the rate: threads that live inside capture_stack most of the time (the continuously looping workers) are the likelier ones to be caught holding it, while the short-lived idle threads of the FLS repro are rarely caught."))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,'The fix is one call. capture_stack uses try_lock instead of lock. If the lock is held, "orphaned or merely contended, indistinguishable and both handled identically", the capture returns empty and unresolved instead of blocking. It cannot deadlock regardless of why the lock is unavailable, and the uncontended path is unchanged. The cost is that a contended capture records an allocation without a stack. The fix was committed as b5c5aed with two unit tests in capture.rs; it removed the #[ignore] that deff3ad had put on the hang test, because an unconditionally asserted hang test "would otherwise non-deterministically hang the test suite and leak unkillable processes on every run", in deff3ad\u2019s own message.'))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"Before the fix the hang was reproduced 9 of 10 and 7 of 8 times; after, the comment records 25 clean manual runs."))
