B.a.h(n,A.b("unit tests for the test helper"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"crates/heaplens-daemon/tests/hook_self_load_spawned_thread.rs \xb7 lines 155-183","    #[test]\n    fn a_single_alloc_matching_expected_size_has_no_mismatch() {\n        let events = vec![alloc_ev(0x100, 32, 1)];\n        let expected = HashMap::from([(0x100, 32)]);\n        assert_eq!(find_alloc_size_mismatches(&events, &expected), vec![]);\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn a_single_alloc_with_wrong_size_is_a_real_mismatch() {\n        let events = vec![alloc_ev(0x100, 40, 1)];\n        let expected = HashMap::from([(0x100, 32)]);\n        assert_eq!(find_alloc_size_mismatches(&events, &expected), vec![(0x100, 32, 40)]);\n    }\n\n    /// The exact confirmed scenario: an incidental alloc+free (stdout\n    /// buffering, size 30) reuses an address before the harness's own real\n    /// scripted alloc (size 32) claims it. Must not report a mismatch \u2014\n    /// the *last* alloc event (32) is the one that matters.\n    #[test]\n    fn address_reused_by_an_incidental_alloc_before_the_real_one_is_not_a_mismatch() {\n        let events = vec![\n            alloc_ev(0x27ff25f61f0, 30, 100), // incidental (stdout buffering)\n            free_ev(0x27ff25f61f0, 200),      // incidental freed\n            alloc_ev(0x27ff25f61f0, 32, 300), // the harness's real alloc\n            free_ev(0x27ff25f61f0, 400),      // the harness's real free\n        ];\n        let expected = HashMap::from([(0x27ff25f61f0, 32)]);\n        assert_eq!(find_alloc_size_mismatches(&events, &expected), vec![]);\n    }"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,'A test file with unit tests for its own helper is unusual, and it is what the commit 084ddf9 did: it extracted the function "and added 5 unit tests, including one reproducing the exact confirmed scenario". They sit in a #[cfg(test)] mod. Their names are sentences: a single matching alloc has no mismatch, a single alloc of the wrong size is a real mismatch, an address reused by an incidental alloc before the real one is not a mismatch, a pointer never allocated is absent not a mismatch, and events for untracked pointers are ignored. The third uses the real address and sizes from the investigation. Together they pin both directions: the fix removes the false positive and the second test makes sure it did not remove real detection.'))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"They live in an integration-test crate marked #![cfg(windows)], so they run only on Windows too, even though they do not touch the OS."))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.b("edge cases and design decisions"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,'\xb7 Spawned thread only. Attach happens on the main thread, "same as every other self-load harness" in the example\u2019s own comment; only the workload moves. The test does not vary the number of threads; self_load_concurrency_stress.rs does.'))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"\xb7 The sleep-based drain. After the child exits, the test sleeps one second, then aborts ingest. The one second is a fixed margin for the last batch."))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"\xb7 Exact count assertions on a noisy stream. The pointer matching makes exact counts safe; the unit-tested helper makes the size check safe."))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"\xb7 Pipe name shared with the daemon and with the other wire tests."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("what it proves about the whole system"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,'Taken with hook_self_load_wire.rs, the pair says two things: a hook loaded with LoadLibraryW captures exactly the allocations a scripted workload performs when they happen on the loading thread, and it does the same when they happen on a different thread. Without the second, "works on my machine" would have meant "works on the one thread I tried".'))
