B.a.h(o,A.b("the scenario"))
B.a.h(o,A.d("rust","crates/heaplens-protocol/tests/frame_resync.rs \xb7 setup: two good frames around junk",'#[test]\nfn resync_after_absurd_length_prefix() {\n    // Two valid frames sandwiching junk with an absurd length prefix.\n    // The junk bytes must not prevent either valid frame from decoding.\n    let event = AllocEvent::new(\n        EventKind::Dealloc, 0x5555_0000_0001, 0, 64, 8, 1, [0u64; 16], 0,\n    );\n    let valid1 = encode_handshake(1, "before-junk");\n    let valid2 = encode_events(&[event]).expect("well under u16::MAX");\n\n    // Junk: a u32 length prefix of 0xFF_FF_FF_FF (> MAX_FRAME_LEN = 8 MiB),\n    // followed by a few bytes. The decoder must drain one byte at a time until\n    // it resyncs onto valid2\'s length prefix.\n    let junk: Vec<u8> = {\n        let mut j = Vec::new();\n        j.extend_from_slice(&u32::MAX.to_le_bytes()); // absurd length\n        j.extend_from_slice(&[0xAA, 0xBB, 0xCC]);    // padding noise\n        j\n    };\n\n    let mut combined = Vec::new();\n    combined.extend_from_slice(&valid1);\n    combined.extend_from_slice(&junk);\n    combined.extend_from_slice(&valid2);\n\n    let mut dec = FrameDecoder::new();\n    dec.push(&combined);'))
B.a.h(o,A.a(q,"Frame one is a handshake for pid 1 named before-junk. Frame two is an EVENTS frame holding a Dealloc event. Between them sits junk made of a u32 length prefix of 0xFFFFFFFF, far above the decoder\u2019s 8 MiB cap (MAX_FRAME_LEN), followed by three padding bytes 0xAA, 0xBB, 0xCC. Everything is pushed in a single call. The test then asserts that the first frame decodes, the second frame decodes after the decoder has slid past the junk, and nothing is left over."))
o.push(B.b)
B.a.h(o,A.a(q,'The scenario models a realistic way a stream goes wrong on a local pipe: a producer that died mid-write, or a reconnect that left the decoder half-way through a frame. (The page for frame.rs notes that ingest.rs creates a fresh decoder per connection, so ordinary reconnects do not reach this path.) The design note records the decision the test pins: when the length prefix is untrustworthy (zero, or above the cap), "do NOT skip by it"; drop one byte and try again.'))
B.a.h(o,A.b("the assertions"))
B.a.h(o,A.d("rust","crates/heaplens-protocol/tests/frame_resync.rs \xb7 recovery",'// First valid frame decodes before the junk\nmatch dec.next().expect("frame 1 (before junk)") {\n    Frame::Handshake { pid, name } => {\n        assert_eq!(pid, 1);\n        assert_eq!(name, "before-junk");\n    }\n    other => panic!("frame 1 wrong variant: {other:?}"),\n}\n\n// Second valid frame decodes after the decoder resyncs past the junk\nmatch dec.next().expect("frame 2 (after junk)") {\n    Frame::Events(events) => {\n        assert_eq!(events.len(), 1);\n        assert_eq!(events[0], event);\n    }\n    other => panic!("frame 2 wrong variant: {other:?}"),\n}\n\nassert!(dec.next().is_none());'))
B.a.h(o,A.a(q,'The first match proves the junk did not damage the frame before it. The second, labelled "frame 2 (after junk)", is the one that matters: it proves that after consuming the junk the decoder lands on the start of the next real frame. The final dec.next().is_none() shows there are no phantom frames from the leftovers.'))
o.push(B.b)
B.a.h(o,A.a(q,"The wording of the failure messages is deliberate. If the test fails, it says which frame was lost, and the name of the second one describes the exact hazard: a frame after junk."))
B.a.h(o,A.b("the trap inside seven bytes"))
B.a.h(o,A.a(q,'This is the story behind the file. The first decoder, from commit ca4e501, followed the simplest design: read four bytes as a length; if it is zero or above 8 MiB, drop one byte and look again; otherwise trust it and wait for that many bytes. The T6 test was written next, and it failed. Replaying that: the tree of ea9b3b2 with src/frame.rs reverted to the ca4e501 decoder, in a scratch directory, dies at the second expect, "frame 2 (after junk)", at tests/frame_resync.rs line 42 of that version.'))
o.push(B.b)
B.a.h(o,A.a(q,"The reason is in the bytes. The decoder slides one byte at a time through the junk, and the real EVENTS frame follows the junk. At that commit AllocEvent::SIZE was still 104, so the real frame\u2019s length was 107, the byte 6B followed by three zeros. Each window the old decoder sees:"))
o.push(B.b)
o.push(A.f(q,"offset  window bytes   as u32 (LE)    length-only check"))
o.push(A.f(q,"------  -------------  -------------  -----------------------------"))
o.push(A.f(q,"  0     FF FF FF FF    4,294,967,295  reject, above 8 MiB"))
o.push(A.f(q,"  1     FF FF FF AA    2,868,903,935  reject"))
o.push(A.f(q,"  2     FF FF AA BB    3,148,546,047  reject"))
o.push(A.f(q,"  3     FF AA BB CC    3,434,851,071  reject"))
o.push(A.f(q,"  4     AA BB CC 6B    1,808,579,498  reject"))
o.push(A.f(q,"  5     BB CC 6B 00    7,064,763      ACCEPT: a fake header"))
o.push(A.f(q,"  6     CC 6B 00 00    27,596         (would also pass)"))
o.push(A.f(q,"  7     6B 00 00 00    107            the real frame"))
o.push(B.b)
B.a.h(o,A.a(q,"At offset 5, the last two junk bytes followed by the first two bytes of the real frame\u2019s length read as 7,064,763. That is under the 8 MiB cap (8,388,608), so a length-only decoder accepts it, enters NeedBody, and waits for 7,064,767 bytes that will never arrive, because the real frame is only 111 bytes. The real frame is swallowed into a body that never completes, and the test\u2019s second expect fails. The values in the table are arithmetic on the test\u2019s own bytes (the junk is fixed, the length is 3 plus one 104-byte event), and the failure of the old decoder is what the replay shows."))
o.push(B.b)
B.a.h(o,A.a(q,"The fix, in the same commit, taught the decoder to judge a header by length and type together (is_plausible_header) and to cross-check handshake lengths against their embedded name length. At offset 5 the type byte is 0x00, so the window is judged as a handshake, and 7,064,763 is far outside the handshake range of 11 to 65,546. At offset 6, 27,596 is inside that range, but the cross-check expects 11 + name_len with name_len read as 0, which is 11, so it rejects it. One more drop, and the window at offset 7 is a valid events header: type 0x01, length 107 = 3 + 104."))
o.push(B.b)
B.a.h(o,A.a(q,"The file as it stands today has the same bytes but a 168-byte event, so the real length is 171 (AB 00 00 00) and the trap shifts by one: BB CC AB 00 is 11,259,067 and is rejected by the cap alone, while CC AB 00 00 is 0xABCC = 43,980, which only the handshake cross-check stops. The test is a regression for the mechanism at either size, which is why it is worth having the junk sit directly against a real frame."))
B.a.h(o,A.b("what the lesson is"))
B.a.h(o,A.a(q,"Test the failure you fear with bytes that are adjacent to real data. The junk here is not random noise: its last byte combined with the genuine frame\u2019s first bytes is what built the fake header. A test with junk padded by zeros, or junk followed by a frame whose length bytes happen not to line up, would have passed the naive decoder. This one fails because the author put real frames on both sides and looked at what the sliding window actually sees."))
o.push(B.b)
B.a.h(o,A.a(q,'It is also a good example of a test driving a design. The commit is titled "test(protocol): T6 resync after absurd length prefix", yet its stat shows 51 new lines of test against 81 changed lines in src/frame.rs: the plausibility function, the per-type length ranges, the handshake cross-check and the resync-from-byte-one rule for bodies that fail to decode.'))
B.a.h(o,A.b("what it does not cover"))
B.a.h(o,A.c(q,"junk that looks like a symbols header","type 0x02 accepts any length of at least 3, so there is no arithmetic to reject a fake SYMBOLS header. No test builds one."))
B.a.h(o,A.c(q,"a corrupt body with a good length","the decoder re-scans from the next byte if decode_payload fails, but no test feeds it a frame with a valid header and an invalid payload (a handshake whose name_len disagrees with its length, say)."))
B.a.h(o,A.c(q,"large junk","resync costs O(n) per dropped byte, so it is quadratic in the amount of junk. Measured in a scratch crate: 64 KiB of 0xFF in one push took 21 to 26 ms, and 128 KiB took 143 to 147 ms. The test\u2019s junk is seven bytes."))
B.a.h(o,A.b("related"))
B.a.h(o,A.c(q,"frame.rs","the plausibility rules this test forced."))
B.a.h(o,A.c(q,"frame_partial.rs","bytes that are late rather than wrong."))
