B.a.h(n,A.b("why this file exists"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"HeapLens tells you four things about an allocation: it is healthy, it is an orphan, it is hot, or it has been freed. Those four words are the entire vocabulary of the product, and they appear in a canvas, a grid, a detail panel, a counter tooltip and a banner. If any two of those draw the same state in different colors, or give it different words, the user has to re-learn the product in every panel. This file is the one place where the mapping is written down. It came out of the memory map commit: the colors were first inlined in graph_canvas.dart, and the second consumer made it worth extracting."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("the three tables"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"heaplens_flutter/lib/widgets/node_colors.dart \xb7 lines 20-25, 34-39, 44-49","const Map<NodeStateDto, Color> kNodeStateColors = {\n  NodeStateDto.healthy: XynorashTheme.teal,\n  NodeStateDto.orphan: XynorashTheme.coral,\n  NodeStateDto.hot: XynorashTheme.orange,\n  NodeStateDto.freed: Color(0xFF9E9E9E), // gray \u2014 no clear theme mapping, unchanged\n};\n...\nconst Map<NodeStateDto, String> kNodeStateLabels = {\n  NodeStateDto.healthy: 'Healthy',\n  NodeStateDto.orphan: 'Orphan',\n  NodeStateDto.hot: 'Hot',\n  NodeStateDto.freed: 'Freed',\n};\n...\nconst Map<NodeStateDto, String> kNodeStateDescriptions = {\n  NodeStateDto.healthy: 'A live allocation with a known owner.',\n  NodeStateDto.orphan: 'A live allocation whose owner was freed \u2014 likely a leak.',\n  NodeStateDto.hot: 'An owner whose children are growing quickly.',\n  NodeStateDto.freed: 'No longer live; fading out of the graph.',\n};"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"Three const maps, all keyed by NodeStateDto and all total over the four values. The colors are XynorashTheme hues: healthy is teal, orphan is coral, hot is orange. Freed is a plain gray, and the comment next to it is honest about why: no clear theme mapping, unchanged from before the palette existed. The labels are the single capitalized word for each state. The descriptions are one plain-language sentence for each, written to be shown as a tooltip anywhere the state, or a number derived from it, is displayed."))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The sentences are worth reading as a spec. Healthy: a live allocation with a known owner. Orphan: a live allocation whose owner was freed, likely a leak. Hot: an owner whose children are growing quickly. Freed: no longer live, fading out of the graph. The orphan definition is the product thesis in one line. It hedges with likely a leak: an orphan is a fact about ownership, and leak is an inference."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("the shared rule, and its boundary"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"heaplens_flutter/lib/widgets/node_colors.dart \xb7 lines 6-13, 27-28","/// Fill colors by [NodeStateDto], shared by every widget that renders nodes\n/// (the graph canvas and the memory map). This is the single source of truth\n/// for the healthy/orphan/hot/freed -> color mapping; do not duplicate this\n/// map elsewhere.\n///\n/// Presentation effects specific to one widget (e.g. the graph canvas's\n/// orphan pulsing ring, or freed-node fade alpha) are NOT part of this\n/// shared mapping -- only the base fill color is shared.\n...\n/// Returns the shared base fill color for a given node [state].\nColor colorForState(NodeStateDto state) => kNodeStateColors[state]!;"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The doc comment draws a line. What is shared is only the base fill color. Presentation specific to one widget stays in that widget: the graph canvas\u2019s orphan pulse and its fade for freed nodes live in graph_canvas.dart, where the code at the call site multiplies the shared color by the node\u2019s fade only when the state is freed. The memory map uses the shared color with no effect at all. Sharing the color and not the effects is what lets two very different views look like one app without forcing one to imitate the other."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("the chip"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"heaplens_flutter/lib/widgets/node_colors.dart \xb7 lines 55-82","class NodeStateChip extends StatelessWidget {\n  const NodeStateChip({super.key, required this.state});\n\n  final NodeStateDto state;\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) {\n    final color = colorForState(state);\n    return Tooltip(\n      message: kNodeStateDescriptions[state]!,\n      child: Container(\n        padding: const EdgeInsets.symmetric(horizontal: 8, vertical: 2),\n        decoration: BoxDecoration(\n          color: color.withValues(alpha: 0.18),\n          border: Border.all(color: color),\n          borderRadius: BorderRadius.circular(12),\n        ),\n        child: Text(\n          kNodeStateLabels[state]!,\n          style: TextStyle(\n            color: color,\n            fontSize: 12,\n            fontWeight: FontWeight.w600,\n          ),\n        ),\n      ),\n    );\n  }"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"NodeStateChip is a small pill: the state color at 18 percent alpha as fill, the full color as border and text, and a tooltip with the description. The comment states the accessibility intent in one clause: it pairs the color with the word, so state reads at a glance without relying on color alone. A person who cannot tell coral from teal still reads Orphan. The node detail panel shows the same word a second time in plain text, as the raw state name in a field row, so both the friendly label and the wire name are visible."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("edge cases"))
B.a.h(n,A.c(q,"a fifth state","colorForState, the chip and the tooltips all index the maps with a null check. The maps are complete today, but a new enum value would compile and then fail at runtime with a null check error the first time a node in that state was drawn. An exhaustive switch expression would make it a compile error instead. Nothing in the source says why a map was chosen."))
B.a.h(n,A.c(q,"unknown wire strings","not here: NodeStateDto.fromWire maps an unrecognized string to healthy, and contract_test.dart pins that. A state the client does not know therefore renders as a teal healthy node, a quiet failure mode rather than a loud one."))
B.a.h(n,A.c(q,"ghost nodes","a node removed from the graph keeps fading on the canvas for a moment. The canvas draws it with colorForState of the last state it had, which is why the freed entry is not the only state a fading node shows."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("who calls it"))
B.a.h(n,A.c(q,"graph_canvas.dart","colorForState for live nodes and again for fading ghosts, using the state they had when they left."))
B.a.h(n,A.c(q,"memory_map.dart","the cell fill is colorForState."))
B.a.h(n,A.c(q,"node_detail.dart","the chip, the tooltip text for the state row, and the sparkline color, so the line has the color of the node it plots."))
B.a.h(n,A.c(q,"control_bar.dart","the orphan description as the tooltip of the Orphans counter, and the orphan color for the number when it is above zero."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("how it is tested"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"Not in a test named for this file. The pin is indirect and specific: graph_canvas_test.dart asserts that a selected orphan node is painted with a circle whose color is kNodeStateColors for the orphan state as its base fill, then a cyan ring for the selection. That test came from the fix for the orphan-banner bug: ead62bb adds it to guard that a selected orphan node\u2019s fill stays derived from state, with the selection ring purely additive. The commit says the suspected second bug did not reproduce; the test stays as a guard."))
