B.a.h(n,A.b("why this file exists"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The canvas shows shape and the map shows layout; neither can say how large this one allocation is, where it lives, who owns it or how long it has existed. This file is the place a person goes after clicking a node. It holds two widgets: NodeDetail, a field list for the selected node, and NodeSparklinePanel, a small chart of that node\u2019s size over time. They were one panel in the first version and were split in the UI refresh so that each can live in its own section of the right rail."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("the field list"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"heaplens_flutter/lib/widgets/node_detail.dart \xb7 lines 92-110","    final selectedId = ref.watch(selectedNodeIdProvider);\n\n    if (selectedId == null || !nodes.containsKey(selectedId)) {\n      // Selection cleared, or the selected node no longer exists in state\n      // (e.g. a `remove` diff deleted it) -- show a placeholder rather than\n      // risk a null lookup.\n      return const EmptyState(message: 'no node selected', icon: Icons.touch_app_outlined);\n    }\n\n    final node = nodes[selectedId]!;\n\n    int scanMaxTs = node.ts;\n    NodeDto? owner;\n    for (final n in nodes.values) {\n      if (n.ts > scanMaxTs) scanMaxTs = n.ts;\n      if (n.edges.contains(selectedId)) owner = n;\n    }\n    _maxTsSeen = _maxTsSeen > scanMaxTs ? _maxTsSeen : scanMaxTs;\n    final age = _maxTsSeen - node.ts;"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The build watches the graph revision so it rebuilds when anything changes, then reads the node map fresh, and reads the selection. If nothing is selected, or the selected id has left the map, it returns an explicit empty state instead of dereferencing a missing node. Otherwise a single pass over all nodes does two jobs at once: it finds the largest timestamp in the map, and it finds the owner, the node whose edge list contains the selected id."))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"That owner lookup is a linear scan with no index. The wire format stores edges from owner to children, so the reverse question, who owns me, has to be answered by searching. If two nodes both list the selected id, the last one in map order wins, which in a clean ownership tree never happens. At the scale this app draws, one scan per rebuild is a fair price for not maintaining a reverse index that could drift from the real map."))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"heaplens_flutter/lib/widgets/node_detail.dart \xb7 lines 116-142","        Row(\n          children: [\n            Expanded(\n              // The symbol is a real code identifier (a resolved call-site\n              // name), not prose \u2014 monospace here too, same as the field\n              // values below, at a size that reads as a header.\n              child: Text(\n                node.symbol,\n                style: XynorashTheme.mono(fontSize: 15, fontWeight: FontWeight.bold),\n                overflow: TextOverflow.ellipsis,\n              ),\n            ),\n            NodeStateChip(state: node.state),\n          ],\n        ),\n        const SizedBox(height: 8),\n        _DetailRow('ptr', '0x${node.ptr.toRadixString(16)}'),\n        _DetailRow('size', '${node.size}', tooltip: 'Allocation size in bytes.'),\n        _DetailRow('age', '$age', tooltip: 'How long this allocation has been live, in producer-clock ticks.'),\n        _DetailRow('state', node.state.name, tooltip: kNodeStateDescriptions[node.state]!),\n        _DetailRow(\n          'owner',\n          owner == null ? 'none' : '${owner.id}',\n          trailing: owner == null ? null : '(0x${owner.ptr.toRadixString(16)})',\n          tooltip: 'The allocation that owns this one, if any.',\n        ),\n        _DetailRow('edges', '${node.edges.length}', tooltip: 'Number of allocations this node owns.'),"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"Every row is a label and a monospace value, with a plain-language tooltip on all but the pointer. The pointer is printed in hex, the size in bytes, and the owner as an id with its pointer in parentheses, or none. The state row shows the raw wire name next to the colored chip in the header, so both the friendly label and the machine name are on screen. The comment on the symbol says why it is monospace: it is a resolved call-site identifier, not prose."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("an age that does not run backwards"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"heaplens_flutter/lib/widgets/node_detail.dart \xb7 lines 77-86","class _NodeDetailState extends ConsumerState<NodeDetail> {\n  /// Client-side rolling max of `ts` across all nodes ever seen, analogous\n  /// to the daemon's own `max_ts_seen` (see graph.rs:\n  /// `self.max_ts_seen = self.max_ts_seen.max(ev.ts_nanos)`). Monotonic --\n  /// updated only via `.max()` against the current live-node scan, so it\n  /// never decreases even when the node carrying the current max is later\n  /// removed by a `remove` diff. Persisted on the state (not recomputed\n  /// from scratch each build) precisely so that churn like alloc-then-free\n  /// can't make a selected node's displayed `age` visibly snap backwards.\n  int _maxTsSeen = 0;"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"Age is the largest timestamp seen minus this node\u2019s timestamp, with no wall clock involved: timestamps are the producer\u2019s own ticks, and the tooltip says exactly that. The first version recomputed the maximum from scratch on every build by scanning only the nodes currently live. The commit that fixed it describes the symptom: when the node carrying the current maximum was freed by a remove diff, which is ordinary alloc and free churn, the next build computed a smaller maximum and the displayed age of a selected node visibly snapped toward zero and climbed again."))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The fix, 0661725, keeps the running maximum on the State object and moves it only upward, mirroring what the commit calls the daemon\u2019s own max_ts_seen. The regression test is named age does not decrease when the node carrying the max ts is removed. The persisted field is also why the widget is a ConsumerStatefulWidget and not a plain ConsumerWidget; it is the only reason."))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"A limit worth knowing: the maximum is never reset. It survives a change of selection, which is right, and it would survive a new session or a reconnect to a different target, which could be wrong if the new producer\u2019s clock starts lower. Nothing in the file handles that case."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("the ring buffer behind the chart"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"heaplens_flutter/lib/widgets/node_detail.dart \xb7 lines 12-16, 32-49","/// Bounded ring-buffer capacity for the size-over-time sparkline.\nconst int kSparklineCapacity = 120;\n\n/// One (ts, size) sample recorded for the sparkline.\ntypedef SparklineSample = ({int ts, int size});\n...\n  /// Records a sample for [node]. If [node.id] differs from the id the\n  /// buffer currently tracks, the buffer is reset (old samples discarded)\n  /// before recording. Consecutive samples with an unchanged `size` are\n  /// deduplicated (only the first is kept) so the sparkline reflects actual\n  /// size changes rather than every message. Bounded to\n  /// [kSparklineCapacity] entries; oldest is evicted once full.\n  void record(NodeDto node) {\n    if (_nodeId != node.id) {\n      _nodeId = node.id;\n      _samples.clear();\n    }\n    if (_samples.isEmpty || _samples.last.size != node.size) {\n      _samples.add((ts: node.ts, size: node.size));\n      if (_samples.length > kSparklineCapacity) {\n        _samples.removeAt(0);\n      }\n    }\n  }"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"SparklineBuffer holds at most 120 samples for one node at a time. It is a separate class, and not private widget state, so its rules can be unit tested without a widget tree: the commit says so. Three rules. A different node id clears the buffer, so a new selection never inherits another node\u2019s history. A sample is skipped when its size equals the last recorded size, and the doc says why: the chart should show actual changes, not every message. And beyond 120 entries the oldest is dropped."))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The deduplication is not only about looks. NodeSparklinePanel calls record from inside build, a side effect in a build method, and Flutter may rebuild with the same node many times. Because a repeated size is ignored, recording is idempotent, which is what makes that tolerable. Samples are named records of timestamp and size, a Dart 3 typedef."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("a flat line is an answer"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"heaplens_flutter/lib/widgets/node_detail.dart \xb7 lines 229-245",'    // A node whose size genuinely never changes only ever produces one\n    // deduplicated sample (SparklineBuffer.record dedupes consecutive\n    // same-size updates) \u2014 that used to fall into the same "collecting\n    // data..." branch as zero samples and never resolve, since a\n    // single-point line has nothing to interpolate between. That\'s not\n    // "still collecting", it\'s a real, final answer: this allocation\'s\n    // size has been constant since it appeared. Render it as a flat line\n    // at that one value instead of leaving the panel looking broken.\n    final spots = samples.length == 1\n        ? [\n            FlSpot(0, samples[0].size.toDouble()),\n            FlSpot(1, samples[0].size.toDouble()),\n          ]\n        : [\n            for (var i = 0; i < samples.length; i++)\n              FlSpot(i.toDouble(), samples[i].size.toDouble()),\n          ];'))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"This comment records a bug that the code now handles. A node whose size never changes yields exactly one deduplicated sample, and a line chart cannot draw one point, so it fell into the same collecting data branch as zero samples and the panel stayed in that state forever. That is not still collecting, it is a final answer: this allocation has had the same size since it appeared. The code now renders a single sample as a flat line, two points at the same value. Two or more samples plot the index of the sample on the x axis, not time, so the chart is a history of changes, not a time-proportional plot; the bottom, top and right axis titles are off and only the left size scale remains."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("edge cases"))
B.a.h(n,A.c(q,"stale selection","handled in both panels: the detail returns the empty state, the sparkline resets its buffer and returns its own."))
B.a.h(n,A.c(q,"rapid selection changes","switching nodes resets the buffer through the id check, and a test covers switching without a crash."))
B.a.h(n,A.c(q,"chart cost","LineChart rebuilds on every graph revision while a node is selected. With at most 120 points that is cheap."))
