B.a.h(n,A.b("why this file exists"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"A research topic is only useful if you can start reading. The model is asked for a starter bibliography with every narrow topic: mistral_client.dart\u2019s refine instruction even states the shape, \u201cexactly 3 starterBibliography entries\u201d. This widget is where those entries become something you can act on. It turns each one into a card and gives it three ways out, Google Scholar, JSTOR and WorldCat."))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The interesting decision is in the first twelve lines. The links are not links to the works. They are searches."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("the three search links"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"lib/widgets/output/tabs/bibliography_tab.dart \xb7 lines 9-19","Uri _googleScholarUri(BibliographyEntry e) => Uri.https(\n  'scholar.google.com',\n  '/scholar',\n  {'q': '${e.authors} ${e.title}'},\n);\n\nUri _jstorUri(BibliographyEntry e) =>\n    Uri.https('www.jstor.org', '/action/doBasicSearch', {'Query': e.title});\n\nUri _worldcatUri(BibliographyEntry e) =>\n    Uri.https('search.worldcat.org', '/search', {'q': e.title});"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"Each function builds a URL to a search results page. Google Scholar gets the authors and the title joined by a space, which narrows a common title to the right author. JSTOR and WorldCat get the title alone. Uri.https takes the query as a map and percent-encodes it, so accents, quotes and ampersands in a French title cannot break the address. That is the reason to use the constructor rather than gluing a string together."))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"Why search rather than link? The file does not say, so this is my reading. The entries come from a language model, and a model can produce a plausible reference that does not exist, or get a year or a publisher wrong. A direct link would claim the work exists. A search link claims only that this is worth looking up, and lets the three catalogues answer. It also needs no URL from the model, which the model could invent."))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"Notice what is left out. The year and the publication are not in any query. That is probably deliberate, since a wrong year would hide the right result, but nothing in the code records the reason."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("the type label"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"lib/widgets/output/tabs/bibliography_tab.dart \xb7 lines 21-29",u.gp))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The model is told to answer with one of three type strings. The comment in the model class lists them: book, article, primary_source. The switch translates them for the chip. Two details. The fallback arm, _ => type, shows an unknown value as it came, so a model that invents a fourth type produces an ugly chip, not a crash. And the article line has a ternary whose two branches are the same word, which is correct (article is the same in both languages) but reads like a leftover; a plain string would say the same thing. The model class also defaults a missing type to book, so a response with no type gets a Book chip."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("the widget"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"lib/widgets/output/tabs/bibliography_tab.dart \xb7 lines 61-91","                Row(\n                  children: [\n                    Expanded(\n                      child: Text(\n                        entry.title,\n                        style: AppTypography.serif(\n                          fontSize: 16,\n                          color: colors.ink,\n                        ),\n                      ),\n                    ),\n                    Chip(\n                      label: Text(\n                        _typeLabel(entry.type, language),\n                        style: const TextStyle(fontSize: 11),\n                      ),\n                      backgroundColor: colors.amber.withValues(alpha: 0.14),\n                      side: BorderSide(\n                        color: colors.amber.withValues(alpha: 0.4),\n                      ),\n                      visualDensity: VisualDensity.compact,\n                    ),\n                  ],\n                ),\n                const SizedBox(height: 4),\n                Text(\n                  '${entry.authors} \xb7 ${entry.year} \xb7 ${entry.publication}',\n                  style: Theme.of(\n                    context,\n                  ).textTheme.bodySmall?.copyWith(color: colors.inkMuted),\n                ),"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"A card is a column. The top row has the title in the serif face at 16 pixels, which gives it the feel of a book spine, and an amber chip with the type on the right. The title sits in an Expanded so a long title wraps instead of pushing the chip off the card. Under it, one muted line holds authors, year and publication, separated by middle dots. The card\u2019s own container, with the raised surface colour, the 10-pixel radius and the border, is built a few lines above."))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"lib/widgets/output/tabs/bibliography_tab.dart \xb7 lines 92-115","                const SizedBox(height: 10),\n                MarkdownText(entry.relevance),\n                const SizedBox(height: 10),\n                Wrap(\n                  spacing: 8,\n                  runSpacing: 8,\n                  children: [\n                    _LinkButton(\n                      label: 'Google Scholar',\n                      icon: LucideIcons.graduationCap,\n                      uri: _googleScholarUri(entry),\n                    ),\n                    _LinkButton(\n                      label: 'JSTOR',\n                      icon: LucideIcons.library,\n                      uri: _jstorUri(entry),\n                    ),\n                    _LinkButton(\n                      label: 'WorldCat',\n                      icon: LucideIcons.archive,\n                      uri: _worldcatUri(entry),\n                    ),\n                  ],\n                ),"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The relevance line, the sentence that says why this book belongs on this topic, goes through MarkdownText. That is the shared renderer for every generated prose field (its own doc comment lists relevance among them), so bold and italics in a model answer render properly. The buttons are in a Wrap so that on a narrow screen the third drops to a second line. The button labels are plain strings, not in l10n/strings.dart: they are proper names, the same in both languages."))
B.a.h(n,A.b("the button, and where it goes"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"lib/widgets/output/tabs/bibliography_tab.dart \xb7 lines 136-147","  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) {\n    return OutlinedButton.icon(\n      onPressed: () => launchUrl(uri, mode: LaunchMode.externalApplication),\n      icon: Icon(icon, size: 14),\n      label: Text(label, style: const TextStyle(fontSize: 12)),\n      style: OutlinedButton.styleFrom(\n        padding: const EdgeInsets.symmetric(horizontal: 10, vertical: 8),\n        visualDensity: VisualDensity.compact,\n      ),\n    );\n  }"))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"launchUrl with LaunchMode.externalApplication opens the search outside the app. On the web build that is a new browser tab, which matters here: the user keeps the topic open and does not lose their place. The Future that launchUrl returns is ignored, so a blocked pop-up produces no message. The same call and mode are used for links in markdown_text.dart, so the app opens every outside link in the same way."))
