B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"site/guides/neovim-cargo-workspaces/index.html \xb7 lines 78-103",'<body id="top">\n<svg width="0" height="0" hidden aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">\n  <symbol id="i-copy" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><rect x="9" y="9" width="11" height="11" rx="2"/><path d="M5 15V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h8"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id="i-tick" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="m5 12 5 5 9-10"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id="i-menu" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M4 7h16M4 12h16M4 17h16"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id="i-branch" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><circle cx="6" cy="5" r="2"/><circle cx="6" cy="19" r="2"/><circle cx="18" cy="8" r="2"/><path d="M6 7v10M18 10c0 5-8 3-12 7"/></symbol>\n</svg>\n<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to the content</a>\n<header class="site-header">\n  <div class="wrap bar">\n    <a class="brand" href="../../"><img src="../../assets/favicon.svg" width="28" height="28" alt="">xynovim</a>\n    <input type="checkbox" id="nav-toggle" class="nav-toggle" aria-label="Show the menu">\n    <label for="nav-toggle" class="nav-burger" aria-hidden="true"><svg class="icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="#i-menu"></use></svg></label>\n    <nav id="site-nav" aria-label="Sections">\n      <ul>\n        <li><a href="../../#why">Why</a></li>\n        <li><a href="../../#tour">Tour</a></li>\n        <li><a href="../../plugins/">Plugins</a></li>\n        <li><a href="../../#guides">Guides</a></li>\n        <li><a href="../../#install">Install</a></li>\n        <li><a href="../../#docs">Docs</a></li>\n      </ul>\n    </nav>\n    <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/xynorash/xynovim"><svg class="icon" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><use href="#i-branch"></use></svg>GitHub</a>\n  </div>\n</header>\n'))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The body begins with an inline SVG sprite: four symbols (copy, tick, menu, branch) defined once and referenced by use elements, so there are no icon files and no requests. A skip link comes first in the tab order. The header repeats the navigation of the home page with links to the sections of the home page (../../#why) and to the plugins page. The menu is the checkbox pattern from the stylesheet, so it works without script. The brand image has an empty alt because the text beside it names the link."))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.b("the article"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"site/guides/neovim-cargo-workspaces/index.html \xb7 lines 105-112",'<main id="main">\n<article class="prose">\n  <div class="wrap">\n    <div class="prose-body">\n    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol class="crumbs"><li><a href="../../">xynovim</a></li><li><a href="../../#guides">Guides</a></li><li aria-current="page">Workspaces, features, crates</li></ol></nav>\n    <p class="kicker">xynovim guide</p>\n    <h1>Cargo workspaces, features and dependencies in Neovim</h1>\n    <p class="lead">Adding a dependency, picking its features and starting a workspace are chores that usually mean a terminal, a browser tab for crates.io and a manual edit of Cargo.toml. This guide covers the commands of xynovim, a personal Neovim configuration for Rust, that do these inside the editor: new projects and workspaces, <code>:CrateAdd</code> with feature selection, <code>:CrateFeatures</code>, the code action that enables a missing derive feature, and the feature set that rust-analyzer and clippy share.</p>\n'))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The main element contains one article. A breadcrumb (an ordered list inside a nav with a label) comes first, then a kicker, then the one h1, then a lead paragraph that says what the guide covers and, importantly, what xynovim is: a personal Neovim configuration for Rust. The lead names five concrete things: new projects and workspaces, :CrateAdd with feature selection, :CrateFeatures, the code action that enables a missing derive feature, and the feature set shared by rust-analyzer and clippy. The following sections take them in that order, with ids that match the sections of the outline: new, add, features, context, deps, limits and try."))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"site/guides/neovim-cargo-workspaces/index.html \xb7 lines 113-113",'<figure class="term"><a href="../../media/15-rust-new-workspace-tree.png"><img src="../../media/15-rust-new-workspace-tree.png" width="1440" height="820" alt="a new Cargo workspace in Neovim: the file tree with crates, the member and the root Cargo.toml" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></a><figcaption>Recorded from the real configuration on the demo crate.</figcaption></figure>\n'))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"Each screenshot is a figure with a link to the full-size file, explicit width and height (1440 by 820, so the browser reserves the space and the page does not jump), a descriptive alt text, lazy loading and async decoding, and a caption. Every image is a file that the home page or the README also uses, which the spec requires, and the build copies only the media that the pages reference."))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.b("the content is checked against the product"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"site/guides/neovim-cargo-workspaces/index.html \xb7 lines 114-125",'<h2 id="new">New projects and workspaces</h2>\n<p>Three dashboard entries and four commands create projects. They all run cargo underneath, asynchronously, and remove whatever they created if cargo fails.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Dashboard</th><th>Command</th><th>What it creates</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><code>b</code></td><td><code>:RustNewBin [name]</code></td><td><code>cargo new --bin --vcs git</code>, then opens <code>src/main.rs</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>i</code></td><td><code>:RustNewLib [name]</code></td><td>the same with <code>src/lib.rs</code>; a hyphenated name shows its Rust identifier</td></tr>\n<tr><td><code>w</code></td><td><code>:RustNewWorkspace [name]</code></td><td>a workspace with a first member</td></tr>\n<tr><td></td><td><code>:RustNewMember [name]</code></td><td>adds a binary or library to the workspace of the current buffer</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Cargo has no one-step workspace command, so xynovim builds it: a root <code>Cargo.toml</code> with <code>[workspace]</code>, <code>resolver = "3"</code> (or <code>"2"</code> for editions before 2024), <code>members = ["crates/*"]</code>, <code>[workspace.package]</code> and an empty <code>[workspace.dependencies]</code>, and a first member under <code>crates/</code> that inherits <code>version.workspace</code> and <code>edition.workspace</code>. The edition is whatever your installed cargo writes into a new project, read once per session, never hard-coded. The <code>crates/*</code> glob picks up later members without editing the manifest. <code>:RustNewMember</code> refuses outside a workspace with a <code>members</code> list and refuses a name that collides with an existing member.</p>\n'))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The first section is a table of three dashboard keys and four commands. Under it a paragraph explains what cargo does not do: Cargo has no one-step workspace command, so xynovim builds one, and the paragraph lists the manifest it writes. The wording is specific, with the resolver value depending on the edition, the glob that picks up later members, and the two refusals of :RustNewMember. These statements are checkable and the test suite checks that each command named on the page exists in the running configuration, as well as each leader key."))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"site/guides/neovim-cargo-workspaces/index.html \xb7 lines 129-130, 133-138",'<h2 id="add">Adding a crate with its features</h2>\n<p><code>&lt;leader&gt;rA</code> or <code>:CrateAdd [query]</code> searches crates.io and shows version, downloads and the features of the chosen release with their descriptions. You pick features and the target: the package that holds the file, <code>--dev</code> or <code>--build</code>, and <code>workspace.dependencies</code> entries are used when they exist. Then it runs <code>cargo add</code>. Writing a path such as <code>uuid::Uuid::new_v4()</code> for a crate that is not a dependency yet gives a hint and a code action, "Add crate uuid", that does the same and then offers the import.</p>\n...\n<h2 id="features">Exploring and fixing features</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>:CrateFeatures [crate]</code> is a feature explorer: which features are on or off, what each one unlocks, the default set, an "unused?" hint, and toggling in <code>Cargo.toml</code>.</li>\n<li><code>&lt;leader&gt;rG</code>, or the code action "Enable feature derive of serde", fixes <code>cannot find derive macro Serialize</code>: the feature is found in the crate\'s own source and <code>Cargo.toml</code> is edited.</li>\n<li><code>&lt;leader&gt;rI</code> or <code>:ImportName</code> imports a name with the candidates ranked and the reasons shown, and remembers your choice per project.</li>\n</ul>\n'))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The sections on adding a crate and on exploring features follow. The page names the command, the key and what happens next (cargo add), and one list gives the three ways of fixing a missing feature. The text avoids comparison with other products; a spec test enforces a personal voice with no such comparison."))
n.push(B.b)
B.a.h(n,A.b("honest limits and the ending"))
B.a.h(n,A.d(o,"site/guides/neovim-cargo-workspaces/index.html \xb7 lines 147-161",'<h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Project creation depends on cargo\'s own naming rules and on a network for crates.io and the egui template; the egui flow uses a cached copy offline.</li>\n<li>The unknown-crate hint is deliberately conservative: a file with a glob import from a dependency gets no hint.</li>\n<li>New Rust and egui project creation is not demonstrated on Windows or macOS.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id="try">Try it</h2>\n<p>Install the configuration, open the dashboard and press <code>w</code> to create a workspace, then use <code>&lt;leader&gt;rA</code> inside a member to add a crate.</p>\n    <div class="install-box" id="hero-install">\n      <div class="label"><span>Install</span></div>\n      <pre data-copy><code id="install-cmd"><span class="ln">git clone https://github.com/xynorash/xynovim ~/.config/nvim</span>   <span class="cmt"># or ln -s &lt;checkout&gt; ~/.config/nvim</span>\n<span class="ln">~/.config/nvim/scripts/bootstrap.sh</span></code></pre>\n    </div>\n    <p>xynovim is a personal project: <a href="../../">the home page</a> has the tour, the install details and the platform status.</p>\n'))
B.a.h(n,A.a(q,"The page has a section called Limits. It says plainly that project creation depends on cargo\u2019s naming rules and on the network, that the unknown-crate hint is conservative, and that creating Rust and egui projects is not demonstrated on Windows or macOS. The last limit matches the platform notes of the README. The last section reuses the install box of the home page with the same two commands, which the spec compares byte for byte with the README\u2019s. The data-copy attribute on the pre element is what app.js looks for to add its copy button. The last paragraph points to the home page for the tour and the platform status."))
